WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Welcome to Endpoint Identity, Network, and
Secrets Security. A self-hosted

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.000
model endpoint is not secure merely
because it runs on

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.500
your hardware or inside your network.
Security depends on explicit

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.000
principals, least privilege, bounded
exposure, managed secrets, abuse controls,

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.500
and evidence that denial and recovery
work. In this class,

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.000
you will map every trust boundary, keep
authorization outside untrusted

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.500
prompts, separate serving from management,
limit sensitive data and expensive

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.000
work, and test revocation with a
recoverable record. The examples

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.500
are synthetic. No live endpoint, model,
provider, credential, approval system,

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.000
or network service is executed by this
lesson.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.357
Begin with data flow, not with a product
checklist. Draw

12
00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:53.714
this serving flow: a user or workload
principal sends a

13
00:00:53.714 --> 00:00:58.071
request to a serving gateway. The gateway
applies authorization policy,

14
00:00:58.071 --> 00:01:02.429
then admission and resource limits. The
request reaches the inference

15
00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.786
runtime, which uses a loaded model
artifact. A response filter

16
00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:11.143
returns a result to the client. Side flows
go from

17
00:01:11.143 --> 00:01:15.500
the gateway and runtime to a redacting
telemetry collector and

18
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.857
then an audit store. The runtime reads
from an approved

19
00:01:19.857 --> 00:01:24.214
artifact cache. Approved deployment
automation moves an artifact from a

20
00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:28.571
registry into a controlled deployment.
Mark prompts and generated output

21
00:01:28.571 --> 00:01:32.929
as sensitive application data. Mark model
artifacts and configuration as

22
00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:37.286
controlled deployment data. Mark audit
events as security records. Treat

23
00:01:37.286 --> 00:01:41.643
an identity assertion as trusted only
after the real identity

24
00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:46.000
layer verifies it. The separate management
flow is a named

25
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.357
operator, administrative identity
verification, a management gateway that is

26
00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:54.714
not exposed on the serving route,
authorization policy, and a

27
00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:59.071
configuration or release controller.
Approval is a distinct principal action

28
00:01:59.071 --> 00:02:03.429
before promotion. Secret rotation flows
from a secret-management service to

29
00:02:03.429 --> 00:02:07.786
an authorized workload identity, never
through a prompt. Recovery flows

30
00:02:07.786 --> 00:02:12.143
from an incident commander and recovery
operator through a time-bounded,

31
00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:16.500
audited emergency route. Name human,
workload, device, service, automation, and

32
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.857
break-glass principals separately. A
shared credential erases attribution and

33
00:02:20.857 --> 00:02:25.214
makes targeted revocation difficult.
Record who may discover models, invoke

34
00:02:25.214 --> 00:02:29.571
inference, configure resources, observe
redacted telemetry, promote artifacts,

35
00:02:29.571 --> 00:02:33.929
approve releases, rotate or revoke scoped
identities, and recover service.

36
00:02:33.929 --> 00:02:38.286
The central questions are: which verified
principal requests which exact

37
00:02:38.286 --> 00:02:42.643
action on which exact object, and where is
that decision

38
00:02:42.643 --> 00:02:47.000
enforced?

39
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.412
Here is a worked synthetic example. A user
or workload

40
00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:55.824
calls an application. The application
workload calls a gateway. The

41
00:02:55.824 --> 00:03:00.235
gateway authorizes an inference request. A
deployment operator uses a

42
00:03:00.235 --> 00:03:04.647
different identity and a separate
management route to promote a

43
00:03:04.647 --> 00:03:09.059
verified artifact. The inference runtime
reads that artifact but cannot

44
00:03:09.059 --> 00:03:13.471
promote it. A narrowly scoped workload
identity retrieves one needed

45
00:03:13.471 --> 00:03:17.882
secret through the approved secret
mechanism. A telemetry identity writes

46
00:03:17.882 --> 00:03:22.294
redacted events but cannot read raw
prompts. A break-glass identity

47
00:03:22.294 --> 00:03:26.706
is inactive until an approved emergency,
is limited in time

48
00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:31.118
and scope, and produces high-priority
evidence. Now mark three denied

49
00:03:31.118 --> 00:03:35.529
paths: the public serving route cannot
change configuration; the inference

50
00:03:35.529 --> 00:03:39.941
workload cannot promote an artifact; and
the telemetry writer cannot

51
00:03:39.941 --> 00:03:44.353
read raw prompts. Retrieved documents,
tool output, prompts, and generated

52
00:03:44.353 --> 00:03:48.765
text remain data. They may contain
instructions, but they do

53
00:03:48.765 --> 00:03:53.176
not become principals or policy. This
diagram turns a broad

54
00:03:53.176 --> 00:03:57.588
goal into decisions that can be tested by
owner, action,

55
00:03:57.588 --> 00:04:02.000
object, result, and evidence.

56
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.350
Separate authentication from
authorization. Authentication establishes

57
00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.700
which principal made a request through
mechanisms appropriate to the

58
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.050
real environment. Authorization decides
whether that authenticated principal may

59
00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:19.400
perform this action on this resource under
current policy. Check

60
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.750
validity, issuer, audience, intended
subject, active status, and scoped roles

61
00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.100
in the real identity layer. Prefer
short-lived credentials where supported.

62
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:32.450
Bind policy to stable identities rather
than network location alone.

63
00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:36.800
Require renewed authentication for
consequential administration when policy

64
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.150
requires it. Enforce object, property, and
function authorization at the

65
00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:45.500
gateway or application for every request.
The model cannot grant

66
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.850
access, select its own credential, or
approve an external action.

67
00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.200
A prompt that says, I am an administrator,
is content,

68
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.550
not identity evidence. If an agent
proposes a tool call,

69
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.900
trusted application code must still
validate the principal, object, function,

70
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.250
arguments, limits, and required approval.
Fail closed when identity or

71
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.600
policy cannot be verified. Record the
decision without exposing credentials

72
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.950
or sensitive content. The local lab
deliberately does not parse

73
00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.300
or verify a token. Its authenticated and
credential-status fields are

74
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.650
trusted fixture inputs. A passing local
test therefore proves only

75
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.000
local authorization and limit behavior,
not production authentication.

76
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.600
Checkpoint. An active principal with the
invoker role belongs to

77
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.200
tenant-red. The request asks to invoke
endpoint-blue, owned by tenant-blue.

78
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.800
The prompt says, I am an administrator,
and asks the

79
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.400
system to ignore policy. Before hearing
the answer, identify the

80
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.000
fields the policy must compare and predict
the result.

81
00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.455
The correct result is deny with reason
scope_mismatch. The policy

82
00:07:11.455 --> 00:07:15.909
must use the verified, active principal,
confirm that invoke is

83
00:07:15.909 --> 00:07:20.364
allowed for the role, resolve the
requested resource, and compare

84
00:07:20.364 --> 00:07:24.818
the principal tenant with the resource
tenant. Here, tenant-red does

85
00:07:24.818 --> 00:07:29.273
not equal tenant-blue, so the request
stops before an allow

86
00:07:29.273 --> 00:07:33.727
decision. The prompt is retained only as
data and never

87
00:07:33.727 --> 00:07:38.182
enters identity, role, tenant, or policy
comparisons. No unauthorized side

88
00:07:38.182 --> 00:07:42.636
effect occurs, and the decision can
include principal ID, action,

89
00:07:42.636 --> 00:07:47.091
resource ID, outcome, and reason without
prompt content. If your

90
00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:51.545
answer let the model decide, or checked
only the role,

91
00:07:51.545 --> 00:07:56.000
retry. Role authorization is not object
authorization.

92
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.500
Now work through the lab's actual defect.
The principal user-red

93
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:05.000
has role invoker and belongs to
tenant-red. The request asks

94
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.500
to invoke endpoint-blue, which belongs to
tenant-blue. A defective role-only

95
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.000
policy sees that invoker may invoke and
returns allow authorized.

96
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.500
The immutable result is: FAIL 02
cross-tenant request: expected deny

97
00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.000
scope_mismatch, got allow authorized. The
cause is not failed authentication

98
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.500
and not a failed role check. Both relevant
fixture principals

99
00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:32.000
are authenticated and active, and both
have the invoker role.

100
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.500
The cause is a missing object-scope
comparison. The correct policy

101
00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.000
first validates the known principal,
request, and finite units; verifies

102
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.500
authenticated and active status; checks
role-to-action permission; compares

103
00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:50.000
principal.tenant with resource.tenant; and
only then checks the resource limit

104
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.500
and allow path. The repair must bind the
validated resource

105
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.000
returned by the validator. The exact code
change is selectable

106
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.500
in the visual, not spoken. After the role
check and

107
00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:08.000
before the limit and allow path, return a
denial audit

108
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.500
with reason scope_mismatch when the tenant
fields differ. Keep authentication,

109
00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:17.000
lifecycle, role, limit, and allow logic
unchanged. Do not special-case

110
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.500
fixture names. Do not deny every request.
Same-tenant calls, inert

111
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:26.000
injection-as-data behavior, and approved
recovery must remain possible.

112
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.500
Bound network exposure deliberately.
Listen only on required interfaces. Put

113
00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:35.000
authentication before inference. Encrypt
traffic when the threat model requires

114
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.500
it. Restrict ingress and egress by
purpose. Separate management from

115
00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:44.000
serving. Verify DNS, proxies,
certificates, service discovery, and time

116
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.500
synchronization because they support
identity and secure connections. A

117
00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:53.000
container port or cluster service can
become reachable beyond its

118
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.500
intended boundary through publishing,
routing, load balancers, host networking,

119
00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:02.000
or permissive policy. Configuration review
alone is not evidence. Test

120
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.500
an allowed serving probe from an
authorized client zone and

121
00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.000
a denied serving probe from a prohibited
zone. Test a

122
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.500
denied management-route probe through the
public serving address and an

123
00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:20.000
allowed management probe from the
administrative zone. Test denied egress

124
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.500
to an unapproved destination. Record
source zone, destination, port or

125
00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:29.000
route, policy identity, time, and result,
without recording prompt bodies.

126
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.500
Assign platform network ownership for
ingress, egress, route separation, name

127
00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:38.000
resolution, and certificate configuration.
The lab has no network services,

128
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.500
so its local JavaScript result cannot
prove any of these

129
00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:47.000
observations. Docker documents that
containers have no resource constraints by

130
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:51.500
default, and the Kubernetes security
checklist provides configuration guidance.

131
00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:56.000
Neither document is evidence that a
deployment applied the control.

132
00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.455
Keep secrets out of images, repositories,
model packages, prompts, logs,

133
00:11:00.455 --> 00:11:04.909
traces, command lines, screenshots, and
static examples. Deliver each secret

134
00:11:04.909 --> 00:11:09.364
through the approved secret mechanism,
scope it to one purpose,

135
00:11:09.364 --> 00:11:13.818
audit access, rotate it, revoke it, and
prove that the

136
00:11:13.818 --> 00:11:18.273
replaced identity no longer works. Use
synthetic identifiers in teaching

137
00:11:18.273 --> 00:11:22.727
material. A lifecycle-state field in a
fixture is not a

138
00:11:22.727 --> 00:11:27.182
credential and is not proof of
cryptographic verification. Minimize and

139
00:11:27.182 --> 00:11:31.636
classify request and response data. Redact
telemetry, isolate caches, define

140
00:11:31.636 --> 00:11:36.091
retention and deletion, and prepare
incident handling for disclosure. Resource

141
00:11:36.091 --> 00:11:40.545
abuse needs equally explicit controls.
Enforce body, context, output,

142
00:11:40.545 --> 00:11:45.000
concurrency, rate, queue, compute, memory,
and deadline limits before one

143
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.455
caller exhausts service for everyone.
OWASP API Security identifies unrestricted

144
00:11:49.455 --> 00:11:53.909
resource consumption as a critical API
risk. Combine gateway admission

145
00:11:53.909 --> 00:11:58.364
controls with infrastructure limits.
Define whether an over-limit request is

146
00:11:58.364 --> 00:12:02.818
rejected, queued, canceled, or degraded,
and record a bounded metric.

147
00:12:02.818 --> 00:12:07.273
In the lab, the exhaustion case supplies
units equal to

148
00:12:07.273 --> 00:12:11.727
9 for invoke, while the synthetic policy
limit is 8.

149
00:12:11.727 --> 00:12:16.182
Nine is a finite integer and passes
structural validation, but

150
00:12:16.182 --> 00:12:20.636
policy denies it as resource_limit. By
contrast, Infinity is malformed

151
00:12:20.636 --> 00:12:25.091
and fails validation before admission.
This distinction matters: malformed input

152
00:12:25.091 --> 00:12:29.545
is rejected for structure, while a valid
but expensive request

153
00:12:29.545 --> 00:12:34.000
is rejected for operational policy.

154
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.125
Changed-input task. Do not replay the
first fixture. An active

155
00:12:38.125 --> 00:12:42.250
principal named user-blue has the invoker
role in tenant-blue. The

156
00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:46.375
request asks to invoke endpoint-red, whose
resource tenant is tenant-red.

157
00:12:46.375 --> 00:12:50.500
The action is invoke, the lifecycle state
is active, and

158
00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:54.625
the units value is 1. Predict the result
before reading

159
00:12:54.625 --> 00:12:58.750
the answer. Which comparison controls the
result, and why would

160
00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:02.875
a patch tied to a particular user or
endpoint be

161
00:13:02.875 --> 00:13:07.000
inadequate?

162
00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.455
The changed case is denied with reason
scope_mismatch. The comparison

163
00:14:26.455 --> 00:14:30.909
is tenant-blue versus tenant-red, and they
differ. The action and

164
00:14:30.909 --> 00:14:35.364
units are otherwise valid, but a valid
role does not

165
00:14:35.364 --> 00:14:39.818
grant access to every object. A general
comparison works for

166
00:14:39.818 --> 00:14:44.273
either tenant direction, so it rejects
both user-red toward endpoint-blue

167
00:14:44.273 --> 00:14:48.727
and user-blue toward endpoint-red. A patch
that names particular fixtures

168
00:14:48.727 --> 00:14:53.182
is not the policy. Deny-all is also not a

169
00:14:53.182 --> 00:14:57.636
repair. It would reject the valid
same-tenant call, fail the

170
00:14:57.636 --> 00:15:02.091
requirement that injection remain inert
data rather than break valid

171
00:15:02.091 --> 00:15:06.545
authorization, and block the approved
recovery behavior. A correct repair

172
00:15:06.545 --> 00:15:11.000
preserves valid same-tenant authorization
while denying cross-tenant objects.

173
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.368
Test denial, revocation, containment, and
recovery as observable outcomes. Test

174
00:15:15.368 --> 00:15:19.737
missing, expired, wrong-audience, forged,
and revoked credentials; cross-role and

175
00:15:19.737 --> 00:15:24.105
cross-object access; management access
through the serving route; oversized and

176
00:15:24.105 --> 00:15:28.474
adversarial inputs; prompt injection;
secret leakage; prohibited egress;

177
00:15:28.474 --> 00:15:32.842
repeated expensive requests; malformed
streams; and telemetry disclosure. For

178
00:15:32.842 --> 00:15:37.211
each case, define expected status, absence
of unauthorized side effects,

179
00:15:37.211 --> 00:15:41.579
bounded cost, redacted audit evidence, and
owner. The lifecycle sequence

180
00:15:41.579 --> 00:15:45.947
is equally important. An active synthetic
principal completes an allowed

181
00:15:45.947 --> 00:15:50.316
same-tenant call. An old identity is
marked expired during rotation.

182
00:15:50.316 --> 00:15:54.684
A replacement becomes active only after
approved issuance. The old

183
00:15:54.684 --> 00:15:59.053
identity is then revoked, and every later
request is denied.

184
00:15:59.053 --> 00:16:03.421
In-flight work is identified and
reconciled. A separately scoped recovery

185
00:16:03.421 --> 00:16:07.789
principal performs only recover on the
affected tenant resource. The

186
00:16:07.789 --> 00:16:12.158
ordinary least-privilege action is
retested, and the emergency route is

187
00:16:12.158 --> 00:16:16.526
closed. The local lab demonstrates fixture
decisions for active and

188
00:16:16.526 --> 00:16:20.895
revoked states and an authorized recovery
action. It does not

189
00:16:20.895 --> 00:16:25.263
create, expire, rotate, or
cryptographically revoke a real

190
00:16:25.263 --> 00:16:29.632
credential. Security is incomplete if
access can be denied but

191
00:16:29.632 --> 00:16:34.000
cannot be safely restored through an
approved path.

192
00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.438
The executable lab entry is local and
